course: religion-ethics
lesson: 39
title: How to argue: reasons and evidence
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Arguing well is not shouting; it is giving reasons: "I think X because Y". A claim is what you think, a reason is why, and evidence (facts, examples, experiences) backs up the reason.
explain: Some reasons are weak, like "everyone does it" or "because I want to"; reasons about harm, fairness and rights count for more in ethics.
explain: Good thinkers ask clarifying questions ("What do you mean by fair?"), use principles such as "do not harm the innocent", and stay consistent: if something is wrong when others do it to you, it is wrong when you do it to them.
explain: They also test an answer by changing the details (what if it were a stranger, not a friend?) and, when they say "it depends", they say on what.
explain: For a child today this helps at home, in the playground and at Sunday school: when you explain why, people can understand you, and Peter wrote that Christians should be ready to give a reason for their hope, with gentleness and respect.
fact: Giving a reason means saying why you think something, not only what you think.
fact: A claim is what you think, a reason is why you think it, and evidence or an example backs up the reason.
fact: Strong arguments have a claim, a reason and evidence.
fact: Evidence is facts, examples or experiences that support a reason; it should be true and fair.
fact: "Everyone does it" and "because I want to" are weak reasons.
fact: A reason that only repeats the claim, like "it is gross because it is gross", gives no real reason at all.
fact: Reasons about harm, fairness and rights are stronger reasons in ethics.
fact: A clarifying question asks what a word or idea means before arguing about it.
fact: Many arguments disappear once the words are made clear.
fact: A principle is a general rule used in reasoning, such as "do not harm the innocent".
fact: Consistency means treating similar cases in similar ways.
fact: Changing one detail of a case can show what really matters to your answer.
fact: Saying "it depends" well means naming the key things it depends on.
fact: The Bible (1 Peter 3:15) asks Christians to be ready to give a reason for their hope, with gentleness and respect.
exercise: Which turns "Zoos are bad" into a reasoned claim? | Zoos are bad because animals lack space | Zoos are bad and that is all I am going to say about them | Zoos are bad because I said so and I am never wrong | Zoos are bad, everyone in my class thinks so too
exercise: What is a claim? | What you think about the question | The proof that you are right | A promise to do something later | An attack on the other person
exercise: In "We should share because everyone gets a turn, like on the swings", what is the example? | Like on the swings | We should share | Because everyone gets a turn | There is no example in it
exercise: In "We should share because everyone gets a turn, like on the swings", what is the reason? | Everyone gets a turn | We should share | Like on the swings | Nobody likes swings
exercise: What makes evidence good? | It is true and fairly chosen | It is long and sounds clever | It comes from your best friend | It agrees with what you want
exercise: What evidence would help in an argument about school uniforms? | What happened in schools that changed | Which colour of uniform your favourite singer would like the best | How loudly people shout when they argue about school uniforms | The number of times you have said that uniforms are rubbish
exercise: Is "everyone else was doing it" a good reason? | No, many people can do something wrong at once | Yes, if many people do it, it must be right | Yes, but only if your best friend was doing it | No, but "I wanted to" is always a good reason
exercise: Which reason counts most in ethics? | It would harm someone who has done nothing wrong | Everybody I know is doing it this week anyway, so it's fine | I really want to and it would be fun for me | My older cousin said it would be fine to do
exercise: What question would help clear up "Lying is always bad"? | What do you count as lying, and does a surprise party count? | Why are you always so annoying when you talk to me? | Who cares about lying when it is the weekend anyway? | Are you saying that because you lie a lot yourself?
exercise: What is a principle? | A general rule we use in reasoning | A head teacher in charge of a school | A single example from one case | A feeling you have only once
exercise: Name a principle a person might live by. Which is a principle? | Do not harm the innocent | I had toast for breakfast | My bike is blue and fast | It rained on Tuesday
exercise: Is it consistent to hate being teased but tease others? | No, similar cases should be treated in similar ways | Yes, because it is only wrong when it happens to you | Yes, because teasing is fine if you are good at it | No, because teasing is only wrong on school days
exercise: Why do thinkers change one detail in a case? | To see what really matters to the answer | To make the case longer and harder to read | To trick the other team into a mistake | To avoid giving any answer at all
exercise: "Is it ok to break a rule?" "It depends." What makes this a good "it depends"? | It depends on whether the rule is fair and whether breaking it prevents harm | It depends, and I do not want to say any more about it at all | It depends on whether anybody is watching you at the time and might tell on you | It depends on how much you would enjoy breaking that rule today
exercise: Why does Peter say Christians should give reasons with gentleness and respect? | Reasons help others understand, and gentleness respects them | Reasons are only for winning and making others feel small | Gentleness is needed only when you talk to older people | Reasons do not matter at all if you just believe strongly
test: My sister says an argument means a fight. In ethics, what is it really? | Giving reasons and evidence for a claim | Shouting until the other person gives up | Calling the other person names | Refusing to listen to anything
test: "school dinners r gross cos they're gross" — what's wrong with this? | The reason just repeats the claim, so it gives no real reason | Nothing, it is a strong argument because the person is being very honest | It is too short, so it only needs more words to be strong | It uses slang, and slang always makes an argument wrong
test: Leah, who is Jewish, says: "We should give to the food bank because hungry families are harmed when there is no food." What kind of reason is this? | A strong reason: harm | A weak "everyone does it" reason | A weak "I want to" reason | Not a reason, just a claim
test: Ollie says kids should be allowed phones in class. Which is the best evidence to ask for? | What happened in schools that tried it | Whether Ollie's phone is newer and better than everyone else's | How many people in the class like Ollie and agree with him | Whether phones were invented before or after Ollie was born
test: You said it's fine to keep money you find. A judge asks: "What if you saw who dropped it?" Why is this a good question? | It changes one detail to test what really matters in your answer | It is a trick to make you look foolish, so you should ignore it | It changes the whole case, so your first answer no longer counts | It is a bad question, because judges should not add new details
test: oi u can't tell me off for being late, u were late last week. what idea is this kid using? | Consistency, though being late before does not make lateness right | Evidence, because the facts prove that being late is never wrong | Clarifying, because they are asking what the word late means | A principle, because "do not harm the innocent" is about time
test: Max says, "Stealing is wrong, but it's ok for me to take my brother's sweets." What is the problem? | He treats similar cases differently | He has given too many reasons for his view | He has asked too many clarifying questions | Nothing, because family sweets are special
test: Two friends argue for ages about whether a game is "fair". What would help most? | Asking what each of them means by "fair" | Seeing who can say "fair" the loudest | Stopping the game and going home in a mood | Letting the older one decide, no questions
test: Which is the strongest argument? | Littering is wrong because it harms animals; birds get caught in plastic rings | Littering is wrong because my mum says it is wrong and that is that | Littering is wrong, obviously, and anyone who disagrees is silly | Littering is wrong because everybody in my street says so, and they can't all be wrong
test: "Should I tell a teacher a secret?" A friend says "It depends." What is the best follow-up? | On what? Whether someone could get hurt is a key thing | Fine, then we never need to think about it again | It depends means the answer is always yes, so tell | It depends means you can do whatever you like
test: "everyone at school says that new kid is weird, so it must be true." What is weak about this? | Many people saying it is not evidence; you should find out yourself | Nothing, if everyone in the class says it then it must be true about him | It is weak only because the word weird is too long to say | It is weak because only teachers are allowed to judge kids
test: Which is true about evidence? | It should be true and fair, not just what helps you | Any story you heard once, from anyone, is strong evidence | Evidence is only needed in science lessons, not in ethics | The more often you repeat it, the stronger the evidence gets
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the constituents of a sound argument? | A claim, a reason in support thereof, and evidence substantiating the reason | A claim, repeated with increasing emphasis until assent is obtained | A claim, accompanied by a criticism of the character of the opponent | A claim, supported solely by an appeal to its general popularity
exam: Identify the reason which would be accorded the greatest weight in ethical deliberation. | That the action would occasion harm to an innocent party | That the action is presently undertaken by many others | That the action is desired by the person concerned | That the action has been sanctioned by an older acquaintance
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of "consistency" in reasoning. | The treatment of similar cases in a similar manner | The repetition of one's claim on every occasion | The refusal ever to revise one's conclusions | The agreement of one's view with that of the majority
exam: Whereas a respondent asserts that the matter "depends", ascertain the manner in which such an assertion is properly made. | By stating the principal factors upon which the matter depends | By declining to furnish any further explanation whatsoever | By deferring the question indefinitely to a later occasion | By asserting that every possible answer is equally correct and equally good
